{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792435504,"nonce":"6P7Y1xp2Ub5TeNDGlmQr3XnFou4=","message":"Reviewed the user's real-window ASHUI_FRAME_LOG capture at bin/code-open-frames.log. The process was still appending, so saved a stable reviewed copy as bin/code-open-frames-reviewed.log (640 numeric frame rows, through about 155 seconds). No product source change in this review.\n\nThe capture confirms repeated UI-thread stalls. There are 21 frames with more than 250 ms of recorded active work, and 21 frames with reactive flush \u003e150 ms. Work here sums events + tick + flush + draw_flush + layout + list + gpu + present, excluding wait. Since-last-frame includes user idle time and must not be interpreted as an operation's latency.\n\nWorst click-driven frame: #496 at 104279.7 ms (reviewed log line 1321), 757.3 ms active work: events 9.5, tick 0, flush 533.8, draw_flush 0.3, layout 179.6, list 2.5, gpu 28.7, present 2.9 ms. Preceding MouseInput records are at 103395.5 and 103531.8 ms: the next frame ends 884.2 ms after the first record and 747.9 ms after the second. Other expensive click-driven frames include #409 (373.2 ms flush, 147.6 layout), #505 (367.5 / 118.0), #519 (381.2 / 131.3), and #531 (406.8 / 139.9). Primitives both rise and fall on these transitions; the capture does not name files or commands, so it cannot distinguish new tab construction from switching/closing solely from those events.\n\nFrame #549 follows a Focused event and has 270.7 ms flush plus 440.9 ms layout. It is not an identified file-open event. Long since-last-frame gaps (including that frame's 20-second interval) include idle time.\n\nAcross all 640 frames, median / p95 / maximum: reactive flush 0.1 / 3.2 / 533.8 ms; layout 2.5 / 6.8 / 440.9; gpu 3.1 / 6.6 / 64.1; tick 0 / 0.5 / 86.6; present/remainder 13.8 / 23.6 / 106.7. This concentrates the relevant stalls in reactive processing and layout, rather than showing a multi-second GPU submission or compositor wait. The gpu field times CPU draw submission, not GPU execution. The present field includes the remainder around drawing and after-frame collection; it is not an isolated Metal presentation measurement. The sole noframe marker occurs at startup (285.4 ms), window=2560x1600.\n\nNo single three-second active frame is present in this reviewed segment; the log does confirm perceptible half-to-three-quarter-second UI pauses. Follow-up should time Watch.runQueued reactions, native tree flush and layout hooks independently during a tab transition; whole-frame data alone cannot assign the cost to a specific Watch, CodeEditor row creation, CSS processing, or collection inside one of those phases. Keep the issue open and avoid claiming the native build or lazy Explorer resolved the reported delay.","files":null}]}